\nSo (F,3) would flip F4 and F5 (two pieces).\n\nNext, (G,3): row G, column3.\n\nCheck directions.\n\nG3 is empty.\n\nNorth: F3 empty, E3 (@). Wait, F3 is empty, so from G3 north to F3 (empty), so no.\n\nNortheast: F4 (*), E5 (@). Let\'s see:\n\nG3 → F4 (*), E5 (@). So yes, sandwiching F4.\n\nBecause G3 (new) → F4 (*), E5 (@). So one opponent piece flipped.\n\nAlso, east: G4 empty.\n\nNorthwest: F2 empty.\n\nWest: G2 empty.\n\nSouth: H3 empty.\n\nSoutheast: H4 empty.\n\nSouthwest: H2 empty.\n\nSo only northeast direction. So flipping F4.\n\nSo (G,3) flips 1 piece.\n\nNext, (G,5): row G, column5.\n\nCheck directions.\n\nG5 is empty.\n\nNorth: F5 (*), E5 (@). So G5 → F5 (*), E5 (@). So sandwiching F5. Flip F5.\n\nNortheast: F6 (@). Adjacent, so no opponent pieces.\n\nNorthwest: F4 (*), E3 (@). Let\'s see:\n\nG5 → F4 (*), E3 (@). Direction northwest (row-1, col-1).\n\nG5 (row G, col5) → F4 (row F, col4) → E3 (row E, col3).\n\nF4 is *, E3 is @. So yes, sandwiching F4.\n\nSo two directions: north and northwest.\n\nNorth: flip F5.\n\nNorthwest: flip F4.\n\nSo total two pieces.\n\nAlso, check west: G4 empty.\n\nEast: G6 empty.\n\nSouth: H5 empty.\n\nEtc.\n\nSo (G,5) would flip F5 and F4.\n\nNow, evaluating each move:\n\n- (C,3): flips D4 (1 piece)\n\n- (C,5): flips D4 and D5 (2 pieces)\n\n- (F,3): flips F4 and F5 (2 pieces)\n\n- (G,3): flips F4 (1 piece)\n\n- (G,5): flips F4 and F5 (2 pieces)\n\nBut flipping pieces isn\'t the only consideration. We also need to consider the strategic value of the move, such as corner and edge control, mobility, etc.\n\nIn Reversi, corners are very valuable because they can\'t be flipped once taken. Edges can be risky but sometimes necessary.\n\nLooking at the valid moves:\n\n(C,3): row C, column3. Not a corner or edge particularly.\n\n(C,5): row C, column5.\n\n(F,3): row F, column3.\n\n(G,3): row G, column3 (close to bottom edge).\n\n(G,5): row G, column5.\n\nLet\'s see the board:\n\nCurrent pieces are concentrated around the center. Taking moves that lead to more flips now might be good, but also setting up for future moves.\n\nFor example, (F,3) is in row F, column3. Flipping F4 and F5 (which are opponent\'s pieces). This would change F4 and F5 to @, so my pieces would be at F3, F4, F5, F6. That\'s a solid line on row F, columns 3-6. This could be strong, controlling the lower part of the board.\n\nSimilarly, (G,5) would flip F4 and F5, placing a piece at G5. But G5 is on the edge (row G is second from bottom), which might be less stable.\n\n(C,5) would flip D4 and D5, placing at C5. C5 is upper part of the board. But C row is still mostly empty.\n\nAnother aspect is whether the move opens up more opportunities or limits the opponent\'s moves.\n\nLet\'s analyze each move\'s consequences.\n\nStarting with (F,3):\n\nPlacing at F3, flipping F4 and F5.\n\nAfter this move, row F would have @ at 3,4,5,6.\n\nThis strengthens the lower central area. Also, having a solid row here might make it harder for the opponent to flank us.\n\nAdditionally, this move could open up possibilities for future moves on row F or adjacent rows.\n\n(C,5):\n\nPlacing at C5, flipping D4 and D5.\n\nThis would take two opponent pieces in the upper central area. But C5 is row C (third row), column5. Not sure how strategic that is.\n\nBut flipping D4 and D5 would give us control there. However, row C is still mostly empty, so opponent might have moves around there.\n\n(G,5):\n\nPlacing at G5, flipping F4 and F5.\n\nThis would give us a piece at G5 (row G, column5), which is near the bottom edge. Edges can be dangerous because they can be flanked, but if secured, they\'re good.\n\nBut flipping F4 and F5 here would connect with E5 and possibly others.\n\nWait, after placing at G5:\n\nF4 and F5 become @. So row F would have F4, F5 as @, along with F6. So F4-F6 are all @. Plus E5 is @, so there\'s a diagonal from E5 to F6.\n\nBut G5 is a new piece. However, row G is mostly empty, so opponent might be able to flank G5 later.\n\nSimilarly, (G,3):\n\nFlipping F4. So F4 becomes @. Then row F has F3 (empty), F4 (@), F5 (*), F6 (@). Not as strong.\n\nBut perhaps (F,3) is better because it creates a contiguous block.\n\nAnother consideration: mobility. A good move should not only gain pieces but also limit the opponent\'s future moves.\n\nFor example, (F,3) might restrict opponent\'s options on the lower part.\n\nLet\'s also check if any of these moves are corners or lead to corners.\n\nThe corners are A1, A8, H1, H8. None of the valid moves are corners.\n\nBut (G,3) is close to H3, not a corner.\n\nNow, between the moves that flip two pieces: (C,5), (F,3), (G,5).\n\nWhich is better?\n\n(F,3): flipping two pieces in row F, creating a strong horizontal line.\n\nThis seems better because it consolidates control in a row where we already have F6. Connecting E3 to F6 via F3-F5 could be powerful.\n\nWhereas (C,5) is in the upper part, which is less connected to our existing pieces.\n\n(G,5) is on the edge, which might be less stable.\n\nAnother angle: after making the move, what moves become available for the opponent.\n\nFor example, if we take (F,3), flipping F4 and F5, then the opponent might have moves around E2, F2, etc. But it\'s hard to say without deeper analysis.\n\nAlternatively, (C,5) might open up more possibilities for us in the upper board.\n\nBut given that the center is where the action is, strengthening our position there is probably better.\n\nWait, current my pieces are E3, E5, F6.\n\nAfter (F,3), we have F3, F4, F5, F6 as @, which is a solid base.\n\nThis might allow future moves like E2, D3, etc.\n\nWhereas (C,5) would give us C5, and flip D4, D5. But D4 and D5 were opponent\'s, so now they\'re ours. So row D would have D4, D5 as @, which connects to E5.\n\nBut row C is still empty except for C5.\n\nHmm.\n\nAlso, in Reversi, it\'s often better to minimize the number of moves available to the opponent, so taking moves that are less likely to open up the board for them.\n\nAnother thought: