ral column.\n\nAdditionally, having pieces in G5 might help control the lower part of the board.\n\nBut (F,3) is in row F, column3, which is more left-central.\n\nIt\'s still a tough choice.\n\nLet\'s think about future moves.\n\nAfter (G,5), we have a strong column5. Possible next moves could be D5, C5, etc., but need to see.\n\nAfter (F,3), row F is strong, possibly leading to moves like F2, E2, etc.\n\nAnother point: the current valid moves don\'t include any corner-adjacent moves, so securing central control is key.\n\nGiven that both (F,3) and (G,5) flip two pieces and create strong lines, which is better?\n\nPerhaps (G,5) gives more long-term potential because column5 is more central.\n\nWait, but let\'s also check if (G,5) leads to more immediate options.\n\nFor example, after placing at G5, what new moves do we have?\n\nNot sure, but the vertical line might open up possibilities.\n\nAlternatively, (F,3) gives us row F, which already had F6, so extending left.\n\nIn the current board, row F has F6 as @, and F4, F5 as *.\n\nBy flipping them, we own row F from 3-6.\n\nThis could prevent the opponent from gaining ground there.\n\nBut I\'m still torn between (F,3) and (G,5).\n\nWait, another consideration: the number of directions in which the new piece can lead to future flips.\n\nFor example, (F,3) is at the start of row F (columns 3-6), so future moves to the west (columns 1-2) might be possible.\n\nWhereas (G,5) is in column5, which is more central.\n\nAlso, in terms of board symmetry, but the current board isn\'t symmetric.\n\nWait, looking back at the valid actions: (G,5) is a valid move, as per the problem statement.\n\nLet\'s also consider the concept of "mobility": good moves are those that maximize your future moves and minimize the opponent\'s.\n\nPlacing at (F,3) might open up more options on the left side.\n\nPlacing at (G,5) strengthens the center.\n\nIn many Reversi strategies, controlling the center is crucial.\n\nBut in this case, both are centra